                               Review by thockin                                

Summary                                                                         

This is mostly mechanical and looks right to me.  A couple of questions, mostly 
about what happens the NEXT TIME (when the gate is actually removed) and about  
regularity between the various test loops -- some pin the emulation version for 
every case, some only for the legacy cases.  Nothing here changes the API       
contract, so I am fine with this landing once those are answered.  /lgtm from   
the sig-network/validation side; the compatibility_lifecycle files need         
feature-approvers anyway.                                                       

Inline Comments                                                                 

pkg/apis/core/validation/validation_test.go (confidence: 0.70)                  

▌  •                                                                          
▌         featuregatetesting.SetFeatureGateEmulationVersionDuringTest(t,      
▌     utilfeature.DefaultFeatureGate, version.MustParse("1.37"))              
▌         featuregatetesting.SetFeatureGateDuringTest(t,                      
▌     utilfeature.DefaultFeatureGate, features.StrictIPCIDRValidation,        
▌     !tc.legacyIPs)                                                          
▌                                                                             

This pins EVERY case to 1.37, including the strict ones, whereas                
TestValidateServiceCreate below only drops to the older emulation version when  
tc.legacyIPs.  Is there a reason for the difference?  I'd rather see one pattern
-- only wind back the clock for the legacy cases, so the strict cases prove the 
behavior at the current version (where the gate is locked).  Same comment       
applies to TestPodIPsValidation, TestHostIPsValidation,                         
TestValidateLoadBalancerStatus, the EndpointSlice test, and                     
TestValidateIngressStatusUpdate.                                                

test/integration/apiserver/cve_2021_29923_test.go (confidence: 0.65)            

▌  • featuregatetesting.SetFeatureGateEmulationVersionDuringTest(t,           
▌    utilfeature.DefaultFeatureGate, version.MustParse("1.37"))               
▌    featuregatetesting.SetFeatureGateDuringTest(t,                           
▌    utilfeature.DefaultFeatureGate, features.StrictIPCIDRValidation, false)  

So the whole integration "canary" now only proves the OLD behavior under        
emulation.  Shouldn't there be the other side, too -- the same objects at the   
current version, proving that leading zeros are now REJECTED end-to-end through 
the apiserver?  That's the thing GA is actually asserting, and unit tests on    
ValidateX() don't prove the wiring.  (I'm also not sure this test earns its name
any more once the gate is gone -- but that's for later.)                        

pkg/features/kube_features.go (confidence: 0.50)                                

▌  •                                                                          
▌     {Version: version.MustParse("1.38"), Default: true, PreRelease:         
▌     featuregate.GA, LockToDefault: true},                                   
▌                                                                             

LGTM for the gate itself.  Just confirming: locking in the same release as GA   
(rather than GA then lock next release) is intentional?  It's fine by me -- the 
beta default has been true since 1.36 -- I just want to make sure it was a      
decision and not an accident.                                                   

